    fn Map<K: Hash + Eq, V>.put(this: &Map<K, V>, k: K, v: V)
        Set `k` to `v`, adding it if absent.
    fn Map<K: Hash + Eq, V>.remove(this: &Map<K, V>, k: K): bool
        Remove `k`; false when it was not there. Later entries of the probe run move back to fill the hole.
    fn Map<K: Hash + Eq, V>.getPtr(this: &Map<K, V>, k: K): &V
        Pointer to the value for `k`, valid until the next `put`; panics when `k` is absent (test `has`, or
        use `getOrPut`). `let p = m.getPtr(k); *p += 1;` updates in place.
    fn Map<K: Hash + Eq, V>.getOrPut(this: &Map<K, V>, k: K, d: V): &V
        Pointer to the value for `k`, adding `k` with value `d` first when absent; valid until the next `put`.
    fn Map<K: Hash + Eq, V>.each(this: &Map<K, V>, f: closure(K, V))
        Call `f(key, value)` for every entry, in slot order (unspecified). `f` must not `put` or `remove`.
    fn Map<K: Hash + Eq, V>.entries(this: &Map<K, V>, arena: &Arena): [](K, V)
        Every entry as a `(key, value)` tuple, in slot order (unspecified), in a slice from `arena`:
        `for (k, v) in m.entries(&tmp) { .. }`. A copy: later `put`s do not change it.
    fn Map<K: Hash + Eq, V>.sortedKeys(this: &Map<K, V>, arena: &Arena): []K
        Every key, sorted ascending (`K: Ord`, checked where this is called), in a slice from `arena`:
        the deterministic order for output, where Rust would use a `BTreeMap`.
    fn Map<K: Hash + Eq, V>.free(this: &Map<K, V>)
        Forget the storage (the arena keeps it until rewound) and become empty with no slots; a later `put`
        allocates again. Use `clear` to keep the slots.
    fn Map<K: Hash + Eq, V>.clear(this: &Map<K, V>)
        Drop every entry, keeping the slots.
